// std/atomic.quanta — the ATOMIC ORDERING ORDINALS (0.0.259).
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. Since 0.0.259 every atomic access takes an optional
// trailing COMPILE-TIME LITERAL argument selecting its ordering:
//
//     0 relaxed   1 acquire   2 release   3 acq_rel   4 seq_cst (the default)
//
// Those five numbers are a language-level fact, not an implementation detail,
// and a lock-free algorithm written with bare `0`/`4` literals does not say
// what it means. These five names are the whole API surface of the module --
// there is deliberately no function in it, because the ordinal is consumed by
// the COMPILER (at_ord(), emitter.quanta) at each call site, not by a runtime
// routine. Import it and write the order you mean:
//
//     import std/atomic
//     atomic_store(flagp, 1, ORD_RELEASE)
//     let v = atomic_load(flagp, ORD_ACQUIRE)
//
// THEY ARE `const`, NOT `let`, AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT STYLISTIC.
// The order argument must resolve to a compile-time literal, and a `let` global
// cannot: reading a global emits an IR_LOADG, so `let o = 2` at the top level
// makes `atomic_store(p, 1, o)` a COMPILE ERROR. `const ORD_RELEASE = 2` is
// evaluated by the front end (F7 parse_const) and spliced in as the literal at
// every use site, so it is accepted. Written with `let`, this module would have
// shipped five names the compiler refuses -- the exact defect class this whole
// module is meant to prevent, and it was caught by compiling the test row rather
// than by reasoning about it.
//
// THE ORDER IS A LITERAL, NOT A VALUE. `atomic_store(p, 1, ord)` where `ord` is
// a runtime expression is a COMPILE ERROR, not a silent demotion -- an order is
// a property of the program text. A LOCAL `let` bound to a literal resolves
// through its copies and is accepted (this was found the hard way: the argument
// records of an IR_CALL are IR_MOV copies, not value records, so the first build
// of at_ord rejected a perfectly good literal); a parameter or a loaded value is
// rejected.
//
// ON WHAT THE ORDERS COST, per operation, on x86-64 (see at_emit() in
// emitter.quanta for the emission and why):
//   * atomic_load  — every order emits the same `mov`: this ISA's ordinary load
//                    is already at least acquire, and a seq_cst load is also a
//                    plain mov under TSO.
//   * atomic_store — relaxed/release emit `mov`; acquire/acq_rel/seq_cst emit
//                    `xchg` (implicitly locked). seq_cst is the DEFAULT, so the
//                    unqualified `atomic_store(p, v)` is a locked store.
//   * atomic_add / atomic_swap / atomic_cmpxchg — every order emits the same
//                    `lock`-prefixed instruction. x86-64 has no cheaper atomic
//                    read-modify-write, and a locked RMW already provides
//                    acq_rel, which is stronger than any order that can be
//                    requested. The argument is still checked, so a typo cannot
//                    pass silently.
module std_atomic;

const ORD_RELAXED = 0
const ORD_ACQUIRE = 1
const ORD_RELEASE = 2
const ORD_ACQ_REL = 3
const ORD_SEQ_CST = 4

fn init() -> i64 {
    return 0
}
